window.docContents[224].resolve({"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___9___-Detached-Entities--3___6___9___2___-Merging-Detached-Entity-State":{"contents":"The merge operation allows for the propagation of state from detached entities to managed instances associated with a persistence context. Given a detached instance, the merge operation returns a managed instance with the same persistent identity and state as the detached instance, but with a distinct Java object identity.\n\nLet e be an instance of an entity class. If e is passed as an argument to the merge() method of EntityManager, or if the merge operation is applied to e via cascade, a merged object e2 is produced:\n\n* if e is a detached entity, the persistent state of e is copied to a pre-existing managed entity instance e2 of the same identity or, if there is no such pre-existing managed entity, a new managed copy e2 of e is created;* if e is a new entity instance, a new managed entity instance e2 is created and the state of e is copied to e2;* if e is a removed entity instance, an IllegalArgumentException is thrown or a failure occurs later when the flush operation occurs; or* if e is a managed entity, e itself is ignored by the merge operation and the merged object e2 is taken to be identical to e.* If e is new, managed, or detached, the merge operation is also applied to every entity t referenced by a relationship owned by e and annotated with the cascade element value cascade=MERGE or cascade=ALL or mapped as such by the equivalent XML descriptor element. For every such t referenced by e, e2 is set to reference the object t2 produced by applying the merge operation to t.* For every entity t referenced by e via an association, navigation of the same association from e2 yields a reference to a managed object s with the same persistent identity as t.\n\nIf e has a version, as defined in Section 2.5, the persistence provider must validate that e and e2 have the same version, either during processing of the merge operation, or later when the flush operation occurs or when the transaction commits.\n\nThe persistence provider must ignore unfetched attributes of detached instances when merging. A persistence provider is permitted to report an exception if an entity instance with unfetched lazy attributes instantiated by a different persistence provider runtime is passed to merge().\n\nNoteWhen this sort of interoperability across different persistence provider runtimes from different vendors is required, the application must not use lazy loading.\n\n\n\nFormalized SpecificationMerge involves two instances: the source e\nand the returned instance target. We include the result as an action\nargument so that the model can describe which object is returned; the Java\nmethod still takes only the source argument. The source must have been created\nand must not be removed.For a new source, the target must be an instance that has not yet been created.\nThe action assigns it an available identity and makes it managed. It leaves the source\nnew and without a persistent identity.For a detached source, an existing target must be managed and have the source's\nidentity. If the target has not been created, the action first checks that no\ninstance in the context has that identity. It then creates a managed target\nwith the source's identity. In both cases, the source stays detached.For a managed source, the final branch requires target = e: merge returns\nthe source itself and changes nothing. These cases form one action below.action Merge (e target : entity) {\n  require created e\n  require e_phase e ≠ removed\n  if e_phase e = new then\n    require ¬ created target\n    let i : identity :| identityAvailable i\n    pid target := i\n    created target := true\n    e_phase target := managed\n  else if e_phase e = detached then\n    if created target then\n      require belongsToContext target ∧ e_phase target = managed ∧ pid target = pid e\n    else\n      require ∀ other, belongsToContext other → pid other ≠ pid e\n      created target := true\n      e_phase target := managed\n      pid target := pid e\n  else\n    require target = e\n}\nIf a removed instance in the context has a detached source's identity, neither\ndetached branch can succeed: that instance is not a managed target, and its\npresence prevents creation of another target. We exclude this situation from\nthe successful calls represented by the model.Creating a managed copy requires an entity value that has not yet been used.\nMerging a new source also requires an available identity, assigned in the same step.\nIf either required value is unavailable, the action cannot occur. As with\nconstruction and persist, this is a model limit, not a JPA exception. Copying\nfields and checking versions are outside the model.\n\n\n\n","context":"Jakarta Persistence\u00093. Entity Operations\u00093.6. Entity Instance Lifecycle\u00093.6.9. Detached Entities","header":"3.6.9.2. Merging Detached Entity State","id":"/#Jakarta-Persistence--3___-Entity-Operations--3___6___-Entity-Instance-Lifecycle--3___6___9___-Detached-Entities--3___6___9___2___-Merging-Detached-Entity-State"}});